  <text name=\"Bees as Essential Pollinators\"><![CDATA[<p>Bees play a crucial role in pollination, which is the process of transferring pollen from one flower to another. This helps plants produce fruits, seeds, and new plants. Without bees, many crops and wild plants would struggle to reproduce, leading to a decline in food availability and biodiversity.</p><p>There are over 20,000 known bee species worldwide, and they come in various sizes and colours. The most well-known is the honeybee, but bumblebees, solitary bees, and other species also contribute significantly to pollination. Bees are attracted to flowers by their bright colours and sweet nectar, which they collect as food for themselves and their young.</p><p>Unfortunately, bee populations are facing challenges due to habitat loss, pesticides, and climate change. To support bees, we can plant bee-friendly flowers, avoid using harmful chemicals in gardens, and create safe nesting sites. By protecting bees, we help ensure the health of our ecosystems and food supply.</p>]]></text>
